---
// Astro returns BASE_URL without a trailing slash at the root but with one
// under a subpath; normalise so `${base}x` is never `/cyber-cvx`.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import { songs } from '../../data/songs';

const withAudio = songs.filter((s) => s.local);
---
<Base
  title="SUNO Cyberpunk Neuro Experiments — Michal Malček"
  description="Fifteen cyberpunk drum and bass tracks made with SUNO across the same year as the systems."
  back={{ href: `${base}`, label: 'Back to overview' }}
>
  <header class="pt-10 pb-8 border-b border-line">
    <div class="eyebrow mb-3">Nine of nine</div>
    <h1 class="text-[clamp(33px,4.4vw,47px)] font-semibold tracking-tight m-0 mb-3">
      <span class="glow-name">My SUNO Cyberpunk Neuro Experiments</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="text-[clamp(21px,2.6vw,27px)] text-neural-text glow-violet m-0 mb-5">
      Who will win the Neuro Wars?
    </p>
    <p class="text-fg-dim max-w-[70ch] m-0 mb-3">
      Cyberpunk Neuro Drum and Bass Jungle Poetry? Philosophy? Random sparks from the random
      universe? Our Future? or more? or other (alter)?
    </p>
    <p class="text-fg-ghost max-w-[70ch] m-0">
      We leaving this here for everyone to make their own opinion or not, that's also fine.
    </p>
  </header>

  <!-- ── Why this sits in a CV at all ─────────────────────────────────────
       The jungle/cybernetics line predates this page; it was written for the
       header in the theme-song commit and belongs here now that there is room
       for it. Kept in its original wording rather than paraphrased. -->
  <Section title="Jungle and cybernetics" note="a shared vocabulary">
    <div class="card p-5">
      <p class="text-[18px] text-fg-dim m-0 mb-3">
        Jungle and cybernetics have shared a vocabulary since the early nineties — feedback,
        breaks, systems that reorganise themselves under load — so a CV about building one may
        as well arrive with a breakbeat.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17.5px] text-fg-ghost m-0">
        These came out across the same year as the systems, from prompts that were often just
        a title, and the overlap was not planned — which is the interesting part: the themes
        recur because the prompts came from someone thinking about them, not because anyone
        wrote the words. <b class="text-fg-dim">Alterity</b> is a value in the hive's own axis and also
        a song. <b class="text-fg-dim">Non ducor, duco</b> — I am not led, I lead — turns into{' '}
        <i>we lead together</i> by its chorus, which is closer to how the work actually went.{' '}
        <b class="text-fg-dim">Neuro Wars</b> asks who wins and never answers.{' '}
        <b class="text-fg-dim">Cybernetic Odyssey</b> is the newest and the most literal about
        it: its chorus lands on <i>human in the system, keeping it alive</i> — not against the
        machine and not saved by it, which is the position the rest of this site argues for at
        greater length and less volume.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Playing here" note={`${withAudio.length} of ${songs.length}`}
           lede="Three are in this repository and play in the header. The rest are on SUNO.">
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3">
      {withAudio.map((s) => (
        <div class="card p-4">
          <b class="block text-[19px] text-fg font-semibold mb-2">{s.title}</b>
          <audio controls preload="none" class="w-full" src={`${base}music/${s.local}`}>
            <a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open on SUNO</a>
          </audio>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- ── Lyrics ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Verbatim from the model's output, in the order they were made. Collapsed by
       default: fifteen sets of lyrics open at once is a wall, and the point of
       the page is that a reader can pick one. -->
  <Section title="All fifteen, in the order they were made" note={`${songs.length}`}
           lede="Lyrics as the model produced them. Open one.">
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-2.5">
      {songs.map((s, i) => (
        <details class="card song">
          <summary class="flex items-baseline gap-3 cursor-pointer list-none p-4">
            <span class="song-n shrink-0 tabular-nums">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <b class="text-[19px] text-fg font-semibold flex-1 min-w-0">{s.title}</b>
            {s.local && <span class="text-[17px] text-synapse shrink-0">in repo</span>}
            <span class="text-[17px] text-fg-ghost shrink-0 song-caret">open</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4">
            <a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
               class="chip no-underline inline-block mb-3">listen on SUNO →</a>
            <pre class="song-lyrics">{s.lyrics}</pre>
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Source" note="one account">
    <a href="https://suno.com/@neonfire" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
       class="card p-5 no-underline flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 flex-wrap group">
      <span>
        <b class="text-[19px] text-fg font-semibold">neonfire on SUNO</b>
        <span class="block text-[17.5px] text-fg-dim mt-1">
          Made with SUNO. The model wrote both the music and the words — most of these
          started from a two-word prompt, the title, and sometimes a style. Correcting an
          earlier line here that credited me with the lyrics: I did not write them.
        </span>
      </span>
      <span class="text-[17px] text-synapse transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-0.5">open →</span>
    </a>
  </Section>
</Base>

<style>
  .song-n {
    font-size: 17px; color: var(--color-fg-ghost);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .song summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .song[open] .song-caret { color: var(--color-synapse); }
  .song[open] .song-caret::after { content: ' ▾'; }
  .song:not([open]) .song-caret::after { content: ' ▸'; }

  /* Lyrics keep their line breaks but must wrap rather than scroll the page:
     a few lines are long enough to overflow a phone otherwise. */
  .song-lyrics {
    font-family: inherit; font-size: 17.5px; line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--color-fg-dim); margin: 0;
    white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
